LOW EXPECTATIONS — TODO
=======================

PRE-EXISTING
------------
1) cleanup the auth.js frontend backend middleware and authUtils......
2) [DONE 2026-10-01] The inviteDevs email flow.

   backend/lib/mailer.js sends invitation mail, wired into all three places a
   link is produced: generate-invite, the invitee's request-reissue, and the
   admin's :id/reissue.

   GRACEFUL DEGRADATION is the design. With no SMTP configured the application
   behaves exactly as it did before email existed — the link comes back in the
   API response and the inviter passes it on. Turning email on is a
   configuration change, not a code change, so a fresh clone without
   credentials still works rather than failing when someone first tries to
   invite anybody.

   A SEND FAILURE IS NOT A REQUEST FAILURE. If the mail bounces the invitation
   is still real, the request still succeeds, and the caller is told the mail
   did not go — with the link, so they can act. Rolling the invitation back
   because a mail server was down would be the wrong trade.

   Config in .env; .env.example documents it and is committed (the only .env*
   file that is). SMTP_USER and SMTP_PASS are optional as a pair — a local
   relay often needs no auth — but setting only one is refused rather than
   letting the relay reject every message with a confusing error.

   Verified across all three states rather than just the happy one:
     * not configured   -> invitation created, emailSent false, link returned
     * relay working    -> delivered; subject, plain-text part and an INTACT
                           link all confirmed against a stub SMTP server
     * relay unreachable-> request still 200, failure named, link returned,
                           and the operator gets a log line

   A NOTE FROM THAT TESTING: the body is quoted-printable, so the link appears
   in the raw stream as `?token=3D...` with a soft line break inside it.
   Searching the raw message for a literal URL fails while the mail is
   perfectly correct — the assertion had to decode first, as a mail client
   does. Worth remembering before anyone "fixes" a link-detection problem that
   is not there.

   Still open from the original note: nothing. The hardcoded localhost:5173 is
   gone too — links are built from CLIENT_URL.


COMPLETED 2026-09-29 (code review items 4, 5, 7, 8, 9)
------------------------------------------------------
  * JSON 404 handler added on /api, so an unknown API route no longer falls
    through to the SPA fallback and returns index.html with a 200.
    CORRECTION: the SPA fallback itself was NOT broken. '/*splat' is correct
    for the Express 5 that backend/node_modules actually provides, and the
    review's claim that it "never matches" was reached by testing against the
    ROOT node_modules (Express 4), which the server never loads. That change
    was reverted; see the dependency section below.
  * JWT identity standardised on the `sub` claim, and authenticateToken now
    re-reads role + is_active from the DB on every request, so deactivating or
    demoting an account takes effect immediately. All `userId ?? id` fallbacks
    removed from backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js.
  * JWT fallback secret removed; server.js refuses to start without JWT_SECRET.
    Verified: a token forged with the old 'fallback_secret' is now rejected.
  * POST /api/auth/logout added; cookie flags aligned between set and clear.
  * Build/serve path fixed: vite outDir is now 'dist' (was '../dist', which
    wrote to <repo>/dist where nothing read it). routes/views.js /app/developer
    pointed at a non-existent developerPanel.html; now devDashboard.html.
    build.js double-slash in the admin stylesheet href fixed; shopfront.html had
    '/styles.css' instead of '/styles/styles.css'.
  * Generated pages are now build artifacts: .gitignore covers
    frontend/dist/, frontend/{admin,superuser,dev}Dashboard.html, and the DB.
    `npm run dev` / `npm run build` regenerate them via predev/prebuild.
  * backend/database.db untracked (still on disk). NOTE: history is not purged —
    commit 240f34c still contains a copy. Passwords are dummy, so this is
    probably acceptable, but rotating history needs git filter-repo/BFG.
  * REGRESSION (introduced and fixed same day): the /api JSON 404 and the SPA
    catch-all were registered in the middle of server.js, above the
    login-step1/step2, OAuth, invite, registration, 2FA and authenticated
    /api/superuser routes. Every one of them became unreachable, and login
    failed with a "Not found" body — the catch-all's own 404. Both handlers now
    sit in section 8, last in the file, with scripts/check-route-order.js
    (npm run check:routes) as a guard.
  * /api/superuser was mounted twice — once without an outer guard at the top
    of the route list, once with authenticateToken further down where it was
    shadowed and therefore dead. Now mounted once, with authenticateToken
    applied at the mount. routes/superuser.js keeps its own internal
    router.use() guard as well, so the routes are protected either way.
  * multer 2.2.0 -> 2.4.0, clearing its advisories (DoS via crafted multipart
    field names, file-descriptor leak and orphaned disk writes on aborted
    uploads, plus a file-size-limit bypass via an async fileFilter race). This
    is the module behind the upload route, so it affected live code. Verified
    after the bump: single-file upload, multi-file upload across directories,
    an empty upload (400 from the handler, no crash), and SHA-256 dedup (one
    stored_files row shared by three version references).

  CONSEQUENCES TO EXPECT:
  * Every existing session is invalidated — old tokens carry `userId`/`id`, not
    `sub`. Everyone must log in again. This is intentional.
  * Logout only clears the cookie for the current browser. It is a stateless
    JWT, so an already-copied token stays valid until it expires. See item 10.


PHASE 0 COMPLETE 2026-09-29 (asset repository: unblock + reconcile)
------------------------------------------------------------------
The creative-approval flow had THREE stacked bugs, so no asset had ever
reached the game store — `assets`, `asset_versions` and `creative_approvals`
were all empty. All three are fixed and the flow now runs end to end.

  1. backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js — the request-approval route built a
     transaction with db.transaction() and never invoked it. db.transaction()
     RETURNS a function; the result was passed straight to .get(), so the insert
     never ran and the bind failed with "can only bind numbers, strings,
     bigints, buffers, and null". Every other transaction in the file is called
     (createAssetVersion.immediate(), storeResults(), reject(), and the
     immediately-invoked `})()` in approve). Now `const approvalId = submit();`.

  2. backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js — `INSERT INTO assets (name,
     asset_type, description) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)`: 3 columns, 4 placeholders.
     Now 3.

  3. Schema drift — the live `assets` table still had owner_id NOT NULL with an
     FK to users(id), removed from db.js in 240f34c. Fixing (2) alone was not
     enough: the corrected INSERT then failed with "NOT NULL constraint failed:
     assets.owner_id". ALTER TABLE DROP COLUMN cannot repair it because SQLite
     refuses to drop a column named in a foreign key definition.

Also found and fixed: the live `invitations` table was missing
CHECK(reissue_count <= 6). applySchemaPatches() adds that column via
ADD COLUMN without the CHECK, while db.js's CREATE TABLE declares one — the
database accepted reissue_count = 99. Not exploitable (inviteDevs.js enforces
the limit in application code) but db.js was claiming a guarantee the database
did not provide. Fixed in both places: the migration now includes the CHECK,
and the live table was rebuilt in place (12-step rebuild, the single invite row
preserved).

How: backend/reset/reconcileAssetSchema.js — drops the 13 asset-domain tables
and lets db.js recreate them (that data was disposable: one workspace, one
version, one file), then rebuilds invitations with the constraint. It asserts
before/after row counts, integrity_check, foreign_key_check, and proves each
constraint actually rejects bad values rather than assuming it does.

Verified: the live database now matches a fresh db.js build exactly — 18 tables,
0 column or index differences, identical constraint enforcement. users (3) and
invitations (1) preserved.

Full flow proven end to end: upload -> technical check (passed) -> request
approval -> admin approves -> game asset "OakTree" v1 in the store, its bytes
shared with the workspace copy via one stored_files row rather than duplicated.

NOTE for future schema changes: CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS is a no-op on an
existing table and migrations only ever ADD COLUMN. So a column removed from
db.js does NOT disappear from a live database, and a constraint added to a
CREATE TABLE never appears on a table created earlier. Nothing detected that.

There is now a check:

    npm run check:schema      # scripts/check-schema-drift.js

It builds a scratch database from db.js and compares columns, foreign keys,
CHECK constraints and indexes. Run it before and after any schema change. It
compares STRUCTURE, not DDL text — SQLite rewrites stored DDL after ALTER TABLE
or RENAME, so semantically identical tables differ by table-name quoting and by
whitespace inside CHECK clauses, and a text comparison reports false positives.

It found MORE drift than the first pass did, which is the point. The initial
hand comparison looked at column names only and reported "one table, two
columns". The full check reports 5 differences across 3 tables, because it also
compares foreign keys and indexes. Two had never been spotted:

  assets.idx_assets_owner                  an index on the stale owner_id
                                           column, never declared in db.js
  asset_versions.created_by -> users(id)   a foreign key db.js does not declare

Both are now gone — the reconciliation recreated those tables from db.js — but
they are worth recording, because they show how much a column-only comparison
misses. Treat "the schema looks fine" as unverified until check:schema says so.

PHASE 1 COMPLETE 2026-09-29 (make the state visible)
---------------------------------------------------
Everything Phase 1 added reads data that was already in the database and had
never been read back. No schema change, so check:schema stayed green throughout.

  * GET /assets now returns, per version: creative_locked, locked_at,
    repository_version_id, latest_check (with reasons) and latest_approval
    (with the reviewer's comment). Per asset: has_pending_approval.
    It also no longer console.logs the entire workspace on every request.

  * GET /assets/:assetId/versions/:versionId/checks — full technical-check
    history, newest first, each run with its individual results.

  * GET /creative-approvals?status=pending — admin/superuser review queue,
    defaulting to pending, ?status=all for everything. Returns the requesting
    developer and the submitted files. Until this existed there was no way to
    discover a pending approval — approve/reject take an id and nothing listed
    them, so the workflow could not be driven at all.

  * The freeze rule: while a creative approval is pending, the asset accepts no
    changes at all — no new version, no metadata edit — enforced inside the
    upload write transaction so it cannot race a concurrent request, and
    returning 409 with a message naming the asset. The submitted version was
    already locked via creative_locked; this extends the freeze to the asset
    around it. Surfaced in the panel as "Under review — frozen".

  * Developer panel renders per-version state: approval badge (Awaiting review /
    Approved / Rejected / Cancelled), technical check pass/fail, Locked, In game
    store, the failure reasons, and the reviewer's comment. Built with
    createElement/textContent like the rest of that renderer.

Verified: 32 assertions against a copy, covering the happy path, the freeze
(including that it lifts once decided), the queue's role gating and status
validation, and the "why" — deleting a file's bytes and confirming the panel
data reads "Physical file is missing or its size does not match: X".

STILL MISSING, and it limits how useful the review queue is: there is still no
download endpoint, so an approver sees the asset name, version note and file
list but cannot look at the actual bytes. Reviewing a submission is therefore
still blind. That is Phase 3.1 — the download route, which is the first code
that would serve user-uploaded bytes and needs care (octet-stream and
Content-Disposition: attachment, never the stored mime_type, or an uploaded
.svg/.html becomes stored XSS on this origin).

Also still missing: no UI buttons to request an approval or to approve/reject —
the endpoints exist and are now discoverable, but both actions are API-only.

NOTE on verification: the frontend changes are confirmed by a clean vite build
and by review, not by clicking. The rendering has not been viewed in a browser.


FILE ACCESS 2026-10-01 (manifest + per-file download)
----------------------------------------------------
The first code in this project that serves user-uploaded bytes. Two endpoints,
both on the workspace router:

  GET /assets/:assetId/versions/:versionId/manifest
      Every file with the path it belongs at, its sha256, size and mime, plus a
      ready-made url per file. This is what a client needs to sync a working
      copy: content is addressed by hash, so a client already holding a file
      can skip it.

  GET /assets/:assetId/versions/:versionId/files/:fileId/content
      Streams one file.

Why this shape rather than a zip: a game asset tree can exceed 4 GB, and a
developer with a working copy wants only what changed. A single archive is the
wrong primary mechanism. It may come later as a convenience for someone
without a working copy; no archive library is installed and the format decision
is deliberately deferred.

Security, all verified by test:
  - the filesystem path is built from stored_files.storage_key ONLY, after
    validating it as 64 hex characters. Nothing client-supplied reaches the
    path, so there is no traversal surface.
  - application/octet-stream with Content-Disposition: attachment, NEVER the
    stored mime_type. Serving a developer-supplied .svg or .html inline from
    this origin would be stored XSS against whoever opened it.
  - Content-Disposition is built from a developer-supplied filename, so quotes
    and CR/LF are stripped rather than escaped. RFC 5987 carries the real name.
  - relative_path is re-validated on the way out. It was normalised at upload,
    but the manifest tells a client where to WRITE, so an unsafe path is
    reported in unsafe_paths rather than passed on silently.
  - a malformed storage_key, or content missing from the store, is refused with
    an explicit message rather than a bare 404.

AUTHORIZATION: the caller's OWN workspace, and nothing else.

There is deliberately NO admin bypass. An admin or superuser who needs to see
inside a developer's workspace does it by IMPERSONATION — marked, time-bounded,
audit-logged. A standing "admins can read anything" exception would make
impersonation pointless and the sandbox not private.

An earlier revision of this code allowed admin/superuser to read versions that
had been SUBMITTED for review. That was removed: it was still a standing
exception, and it quietly reopened the door impersonation exists to control.

Consequence, worth stating plainly: an approver cannot fetch the bytes of a
submission without impersonating. If reviewing files becomes routine, the
answer is impersonation — not an exception here.

A missing version and someone else's version both return 404, not 403, so this
cannot be used to probe for the existence of another developer's work.

Verified with 26 assertions, most of them negative cases: cross-developer
access, admin access before vs after submission, malformed storage_key,
missing content, traversal in relative_path, and header injection through a
filename containing quotes and newlines.


INVITATIONS 2026-10-01 (backend done, delivery not)
---------------------------------------------------
Testers are NOT a role. A tester is a plain `user` — a login with no extra
privileges — because the tester features (download a build, comment, report)
are available to anyone logged in. So `user` is invitable, and is the default
invitation role as the least privileged. frontend/user.html is `<h1> User
Hello </h1>`; that placeholder IS the current tester experience.

SECURITY FIX — admin-to-superuser escalation, now closed.
Both invitation endpoints took assigned_role straight from the request body
behind requireAdmin, and returned the token in the response. So any admin
could mint a superuser invitation and redeem it themselves in two requests:

    POST /api/auth/create-invite    { assigned_role: 'superuser' } -> token
    POST /api/auth/register-invited { ..., inviteToken: token }    -> superuser

The rule now: an inviter may assign only roles STRICTLY BELOW their own
(backend/lib/roles.js). A superuser cannot invite another superuser, so there
can only ever be one — deliberate, but it means no backup exists. The seeded
superuser is created by db.js when users is empty, so the system cannot be left
with none.

  * POST /api/auth/create-invite REMOVED. One place creates invitations now:
    /api/inviteDevs/generate-invite.
  * GET /api/inviteDevs/assignable-roles — so the UI offers only valid options.
  * POST /api/inviteDevs/:id/revoke — new. A USED invitation is deliberately
    not revocable (409): the account exists and revoking the invitation would
    not affect it, so "revoked" would be a lie. The message points at
    deactivating the user instead.
  * Registration now refuses with a REASON (revoked / used / expired) instead
    of reporting all three as "invalid or expired".
  * Reissue returns the new link. It used to mint a token and return only
    success/expiry, so the call worked and the developer received nothing.

SCHEMA: invitations rebuilt — `is_used` became a three-state `status`
(pending / used / revoked), and `assigned_role` gained 'user'. Both are CHECK
constraints, which SQLite cannot alter, so it is a rebuild in db.js's
applySchemaPatches, guarded on the old column name and idempotent. EXPIRY IS
NOT A STATE — it is derived from expires_at on read, so no scheduled job is
needed and a missed job cannot leave an invitation stuck. Applies to every
role; 28 days and the 6-reissue cap are unchanged.

Verified with 32 assertions, weighted at the security end: an admin is refused
a superuser invitation AND gets no token back; same for admin-at-own-level and
superuser-at-own-level; an admin can invite developer and user; a superuser can
invite an admin. Plus status, revocation, expiry, and the duplicate endpoint
returning 404.

REGISTER PAGE — built 2026-10-01.

  /register?token=... is a real page now (frontend/register.html, built from
  components/registerForm.html + src/register.js). It is NOT behind the app
  shell: whoever arrives has no account, which is the whole reason they are
  there. Four states, only one of which is a form:

    pending   the form
    expired   the "Invitation Expired" screen with a reissue button
    revoked   withdrawn by an administrator
    used      already accepted — point at login
    unknown   not a valid link

  Backed by GET /api/inviteDevs/token-state?token=... — unauthenticated by
  necessity, since the caller has no account. Discovering a token this way is
  not a risk: they are 32 random bytes.

  THE INVITE-EXPIRED SCREEN WAS IN THE WRONG PLACE and has MOVED here from the
  developer dashboard. It could never have worked where it was: an
  expired-invite holder has no account, so they can never be logged in to see
  it. It was also broken three more ways on that page — its button called
  handleDeveloperReissueRequest(), an export of auth.js which was not loaded
  there (ReferenceError), and it fetched /api/invites/request-reissue, a route
  that has never existed. And it dragged in an unrelated, entirely dead
  "Developer Tools & Sandbox" section. All of it is gone from the dashboard;
  developerInviteExp.html and .js are deleted.

STILL MISSING — the flow is reachable but not yet self-service:

  1. NO EMAIL. Creation and reissue both return the link in the API response as
     a stopgap, so the inviter has to pass it on by hand. The register page
     shows a reissued link directly to the person who asked, which is the right
     stopgap but still not the real thing. This is the deferred SMTP/nodemailer
     item (TODO item 2); the nodemailer 9 -> 10 bump belongs with it.
  2. frontend/src/sharedInvites.js REWRITTEN 2026-10-01 — no longer a gap.
     It called two things that never existed: a reissue-by-id route, and a
     copy helper reading an element nothing rendered. Neither reissue nor copy
     has ever worked from that panel.

     Fixed by adding the missing endpoint (POST /:id/reissue) and giving the
     copy helper something real to copy — the generated and reissued links are
     now shown with a copy button.

     Also fixed in the same file:
       * the status shown is the SERVER's. It used to be recomputed from
         createdAt + 28 days, which was wrong twice: a reissue moves expiresAt
         but not createdAt, so a renewed invitation read as expired forever;
         and `new Date('2026-09-07 13:08:31')` is an Invalid Date in Safari.
       * STORED XSS. invite.email, role and status were interpolated into
         innerHTML, so an invitation to `<img src=x onerror=...>` executed in
         the admin's session. Every value is escaped now, verified mechanically
         rather than by eye — all 9 interpolations in the markup surface are
         wrapped.
       * the role select was hardcoded to Developer/Admin/Superuser and now
         reflects what the caller may actually assign, from
         GET /assignable-roles. An admin was being offered "Superuser", which
         the server refuses.
       * Revoke added to the panel; the list had no way to withdraw one.
       * one delegated click listener instead of one per row per refresh.
     admin.js's now-unused import of reissueInvite/getInviteStatus removed.

  3. The two reissue paths now share their guards (lib/invites.js canReissue),
     so they cannot drift on what may be reissued.



ONBOARDING DOCS 2026-10-01 (stubs)
----------------------------------
  docs/onboarding-developers
  docs/onboarding-users

Both are STUBS, written from what the code actually does rather than from what
the specs intend, with everything unbuilt marked NOT BUILT. That distinction is
the point: they should not describe features to a reader as though they exist.

For developers the portal is mostly real, so that one is substantive —
invitations, the workspace and its quota, the folder naming rules, what happens
to a version, deleting, and getting work back.

For testers almost nothing exists, and the doc says so plainly. Its main job
today is to record that TESTER IS NOT A ROLE: a tester is a plain `user`, and
the tester features (download a build, comment, report) are meant to be
available to anyone logged in.

Neither is served yet. They should become pages on the portal once there is
something to describe — and the tester one in particular will need the
discussion board and bug reporting to exist first.

Both end with a FUTURE SECTIONS list, so it is obvious what is missing rather
than the document silently looking complete.


GAME-SPACE READ — DEFERRED BY DECISION (2026-10-01)
---------------------------------------------------
DEFERRED, not forgotten, and deliberately bundled with building the game.

A developer cannot currently browse or download assets that have been APPROVED
into the game store. That is how they are meant to pick up their own approved
work as the basis for continuing, and it is why the workspace quota excludes
game-store content — the round trip is the point.

Decision: wait until the game itself is being built. The developer access rules
depend on how the game is actually structured — how assets are organised, what
a working copy looks like, how dependencies resolve between them — and writing
them now would mean guessing, then rewriting once the game exists.

So this is not a gap in the portal; it is an interface that has to be designed
against the game, not ahead of it.

When it happens it needs a /api/game router (layer C in docs/assettypes01):
browse the store, and download its files. Note it introduces a THIRD access
class alongside the two that exist:

    workspace content   the owning developer only (no admin bypass —
                        impersonation is the only way in)
    game content        readable by any authenticated developer
    release management  admin/superuser (not yet built)

Getting that split right is the main design work, and it is why the admin read
path was removed from the workspace endpoints first — the rules had to be clean
before a third class was added.


WORKSPACE QUOTA AND SOFT DELETE 2026-10-01
-----------------------------------------
A workspace is a STAGING AREA, not a store. Two additions that depend on each
other — a limit with no way to free space would be a trap, so they shipped
together.

SOFT DELETE. A developer can delete anything in their own workspace.

  DELETE /assets/:assetId                      (the asset and its versions)
  DELETE /assets/:assetId/versions/:versionId  (one version)

  * HARD delete is not available, and not by choice:
    technical_check_runs.workspace_version_id and
    creative_approvals.workspace_version_id are foreign keys with no ON DELETE
    clause, so a version that has ever been checked or reviewed cannot be
    deleted. That is arguably correct — the history of something submitted
    should not be erasable. Soft delete sidesteps it: the rows stay, marked
    is_active = 0, so review history survives and quota still frees, because
    accounting counts live rows only.

  * Only workspace rows are touched. stored_files, assets and asset_versions
    are never modified, so game-owned content and content another workspace
    happens to share are unaffected. The developer deletes what belongs to
    them — their own uploads and their own references.

  * Refused while an approval is pending (409), matching the freeze rule. A
    version already approved into the game store CAN be deleted from the
    workspace: the game store holds its own copy, and the response says so.

  * NEW COLUMN: workspace_versions.is_active, added to db.js and to
    applySchemaWorkflowPatches. check:schema confirmed the migration produces a
    schema matching db.js. (Note it compares columns as a SET, not in order:
    ALTER TABLE appends, so a migrated column always sits at the end while a
    fresh build declares it mid-table. Order is meaningless in SQL and a
    positional comparison would report that as drift on every migration.)

QUOTA. WORKSPACE_LIMIT_BYTES, default 5 GiB, tunable without a code change.

  usage = SUM(file_size) over DISTINCT stored_files referenced by this
          workspace's ACTIVE assets and ACTIVE versions,
          EXCLUDING anything referenced by asset_version_files (game store).

  * Distinct, because content is addressed by SHA: re-uploading unchanged files
    must not count again, or every iteration would appear to cost the full size
    of the tree. Verified: identical content under a NEW asset name adds zero.

  * Game-store content excluded, because it is not what the workspace is
    staging, and pulling an approved asset back down to iterate must not
    consume quota. Verified: approving an asset drops its bytes from the
    developer's usage.

  * Content shared with ANOTHER developer's workspace is NOT excluded — it is
    not approved, so it is still staging, and it counts for both. Conservative,
    and stops free-riding.

  * Enforced in the upload route BEFORE anything is written, so a refusal
    leaves no partial state. Verified: a refused upload writes no stored_files
    row, does not change usage, and leaves no temp files.

  * 413 with a readable message naming the projection, the limit, current usage
    and the delta. NOTE: the first version divided by 1024 twice and reported a
    2000-byte limit as "0.0 MB" — formatBytes() now picks the unit, so the
    default reads "5.0 GB" and the sample message reads naturally.

  * GET /assets returns a usage block (bytes, limit, remaining, percent) so the
    panel can warn before an upload is attempted. The client figure is advisory;
    the server enforces regardless, so a modified client cannot bypass it.

Tested with 31 assertions across two runs (a normal limit and a 2000-byte
limit), weighted toward the negative cases: dedup, the game-store exclusion,
refusals leaving no trace, cross-developer and admin refusal, deletion blocked
under review, and that soft delete never destroys shared bytes.

PANEL (built): a usage bar in the "My Workspace" header ("4.2 GB of 5.0 GB
used", colour-coded), a pre-flight line in the upload preview ("Total 600 MB
across 12 files") with a warning when the upload would exceed, and delete
buttons on each asset and each version.

  The pre-flight figure deliberately ASSUMES EVERY FILE IS NEW. The browser
  cannot know which files the workspace already holds without hashing all of
  them, which is far too slow at GB scale. So it is an upper bound: if it fits,
  the upload definitely fits. The wording says "may exceed" rather than "will",
  and the server is what actually refuses.

  Delete buttons are disabled while an approval is pending, so a developer
  cannot click into a 409 the UI already knows about.

  NOT VERIFIED IN A BROWSER. Confirmed by a clean vite build, by the new UI
  strings appearing in the bundle, and by cross-checking that all 20 element
  ids the client reaches for exist in the built page — a typo'd id fails
  silently behind an `if (!el) return`, so that check is worth keeping. But
  nobody has looked at it. Worth a visual pass.


SHARED CODE — ONE FUNCTION PER TASK
-----------------------------------
Two places, and nothing should be written twice:

  shared/*.mjs            rules BOTH sides need. The backend require()s them
                          (Node 22 allows require() of an ES module) and the
                          frontend imports them (vite resolves the sibling
                          directory, in dev and in build — both verified).

  frontend/src/utils.js   client-only helpers.

If a rule is needed in more than one place it goes in one of those. If it is
needed in one place only it stays there — moving it for symmetry is relocation,
not consolidation.

WHAT THIS FIXED: the backup-file rule was written twice, once per side, with a
test asserting the two agreed. `$` was defined in five client files and
escapeHtml in three. All identical, all by luck, and the failure mode of copies
is that they diverge silently in the one that matters. A test that catches
divergence is a poor substitute for not having two copies to diverge, so the
copies are gone rather than policed.

STILL DUPLICATED, on purpose: several client files call getElementById directly
where $ would do. That is the platform API used directly, not a second
implementation of anything, so converting it would be churn. Worth tidying if
those files are touched anyway.


ONE DEPENDENCY TREE NOW (RESOLVED 2026-09-29)
--------------------------------------------
There used to be a second, unmanaged tree at backend/node_modules with no
backend/package.json behind it. Node resolved from backend/server.js upward,
so the server ran Express 5.2.1 from that tree while the root manifest claimed
express ^4.19.2, and part of the backend's dependencies (bcryptjs,
better-sqlite3, multer, passport) came from the root tree and part from the
nested one.

backend/node_modules has been deleted and the root manifest now states the
versions that were actually in use:

    express    ^4.19.2  ->  ^5.2.1
    dotenv     ^16.4.5  ->  ^17.4.2

Every backend dependency now resolves from the root tree. Verified: 0 modules
resolve to backend/node_modules, the server starts, and the full behaviour
battery passes (SPA fallback, /api 404, auth, sub-claim tokens, rejection of
old and forged tokens, is_active, login round-trip, developer upload, logout).

Consequences to remember:

  * The backend IS Express 5. Express 5 wildcards need a name:
    app.get('/*splat'), NOT app.get('*'), which throws
    "Missing parameter name at index 1" at startup.
  * dotenv 17 prints the "◇ injected env ... // tip:" banner. Cosmetic.
  * Do not run `npm install` inside backend/ — it will recreate the second
    tree and shadow the root one again.
  * Express 5 sets req.body to undefined where Express 4 gave {}. See item 21.

NEXT SECTION — ASSET TYPES, PREVIEW, LOCAL DISTRIBUTION
-------------------------------------------------------
IN PROGRESS. The upload model is being built (see below).

BUILT:

  ASSET CATEGORY — which part of the game an asset belongs to. A flat content
  domain list (Character · Environment · Prop · Vehicle · Weapon · FX · Audio ·
  UI · Other) in shared/assetRules.mjs. The developer sets it on upload; an
  admin or superuser can change it afterwards.

  NOT A CHECK CONSTRAINT, and this is the point. asset_type was a CHECK, and a
  CHECK cannot be altered, so changing that list meant a full 12-step table
  rebuild — twice. This is a free-text column validated in code, so adding a
  category is a one-line edit and no migration.

  The list is a HINT, not a rule: a value off the list is stored as given rather
  than discarded, because silently dropping a name someone chose is worse than
  storing an untidy one. The form is a select with an "Other…" box so both are
  reachable.

  Stored on workspace_assets AND assets — it is carried across on acceptance,
  because the repository is where someone would look for "every Character", and
  a classification that died at the boundary would be useless exactly where it
  pays off.

  POST /assets/:assetId/category — admin/superuser. This is a DELIBERATE
  EXCEPTION to "admins reach into a workspace only by impersonation": it writes
  one column on one row, reads nothing, and is audit-logged. If it ever grows a
  reason to read the asset, it has stopped being this endpoint.

  NOT BUILT: no UI calls that endpoint yet. There is no admin view of workspace
  assets to hang it on, so the route is reachable by API only. An admin can
  change a category, but only in a terminal. Needs a home — most likely
  wherever the approval queue grows an asset view.

  Also NOT decided: whether a developer should be able to retag their OWN asset
  without re-uploading. It is not granted, because it was not asked for; today
  they change it by re-uploading with a different value, since the upload path
  COALESCEs it.

  shared/assetRules.mjs — the folder-name parser, the three validation tiers
  (block reserved extensions / warn on dotted separators / accept), the
  three-tier match (exact / near / different), deriveUploadRoot() for getting
  the folder out of an upload's paths, isBackupFile(), and the category list.
  It was backend/lib/assetNaming.js; it moved to shared/ so the client uses the
  same copy rather than a second one (see SHARED CODE above). Tested by
  scripts/check-naming.js, 64 cases, `npm run check:naming`.

  POST /assets/check-name — preflight. The naming rules live on the server
  ONLY, so the client asks rather than carrying its own copy of the regex. One
  source of truth, and the preview cannot disagree with what the upload does.
  Returns a machine-readable `code` alongside the human message, so the client
  picks its UI from the code rather than by matching prose.

  The upload route now derives the asset name from the FOLDER, not a typed
  field. bigTree_v04 is asset "bigTree"; the _v04 is the developer's own
  labelling. THE VERSION IS UNCHANGED — still a per-asset counter, MAX + 1.
  It is housekeeping, and belongs in additional details rather than being the
  headline. My earlier worry that stopping auto-versioning would leave a gap
  was unfounded: nothing needed to change.

  The near-match prompt is enforced on upload: a 409 carrying both options,
  and confirmed_asset_name must be one of them. The validation tiers are
  enforced too — a file-extension name is refused (422) with an explanation.

  Client: the typed asset-name field is gone. A folder selection derives the
  name and shows what the upload will do; a file selection with no folder shows
  an asset picker instead (existing asset, or a new name). Both show the
  near-match question and any verdict before the upload is attempted.

  NOT VERIFIED IN A BROWSER. Clean build, and scripts/check-dom-ids.js confirms
  every id the client looks up is declared — that check exists because an
  undeclared id fails SILENTLY behind `if (!el) return`, which is the likely
  way this kind of change breaks unnoticed. But nobody has clicked it.

DESIGN NOTES now settled, recorded in docs/uploadmodel01:
  * The developer's _vNN is their own labelling, NOT stored as a column —
    working folders get names like tryagainStupid and Ihatethis, so it is not
    worth promoting to a field. The SHA is truth for content, the system
    counter is truth for history, the name is a human convenience.
  * No escape hatch from a silly name. It is a sandbox; let them make a mess.
  * Single-file uploads ask which asset they belong to, including a new name.

Still designed but not built. Two design notes, both in docs/:

  docs/assettypes01   upload types, on-site preview, downloading to an
                      approver's local working copy. Maps the work onto
                      docs/Specification_v04_web.html sections 02.4, 02.6 and
                      the TBD vetting gate.

  docs/uploadmodel01  the developer upload and naming model. Amends
                      docs/dbspec01, which is now annotated to point at it.

Read BOTH before starting. uploadmodel01 is the more consequential of the two:
it changes what an upload MEANS.

  Uploads currently CREATE a version on every drop (versionNumber = MAX + 1).
  Under the new model the developer owns versioning in the sandbox and an
  upload record is a HISTORY TRAIL entry, not a storage event. An upload that
  adds no new bytes is still recorded — that is correct, it documents what was
  submitted, under what name, and when. Only genuinely new content reaches the
  SHA store; known content is referenced.

  NOTE the dependency: nothing except the upload route currently creates a
  workspace_version, so stopping it from auto-creating leaves a gap. The
  replacement path (developer creates/selects a version) has to land with it.

  uploadmodel01 also carries: the folder-name parser and its separator
  variants, the three-tier name match (exact / near / different), the three
  validation tiers (block reserved extensions / warn on marginal like tree.v2 /
  accept), the two clash mechanisms (whole-upload warn, per-path
  overwrite-rename-merge-add), and the rule that paths are NEVER rewritten.
  That last one means filename_overrides should be removed.

Read that note before starting. It maps the work onto
docs/Specification_v04_web.html sections 02.4, 02.6 and the TBD vetting gate,
lists the spec-vs-code divergences this touches, and records four open
questions (cloud stack vs local, which preview approach, which types first,
whether to restore recommended_version_id).

Two things in it are corrections to decisions already made, so they matter
before any code is written:

  * recommended_version_id was removed from db.js and the live database in
    Phase 0 (5d3d24f), against docs/dbspec01 which explicitly requires it and
    says it is nullable. The reasoning used at the time -- that a single
    pointer cannot express several production versions -- was wrong, because
    "recommended" (which version to build from now) and release membership
    (what shipped when) are different concepts. asset_recommended_versions was
    left behind, orphaned. Restoring it is cheap; the asset tables are empty.

  * "Run a cutdown version of the game in the browser" is not achievable with
    Unreal Engine -- the HTML5 target was dropped after UE4 and cooked .uasset
    files cannot be hot-loaded. The spec does not ask for it either; 02.4 says
    "WebGL viewer". The three real options (pre-rendered turntables, glTF +
    three.js, or Pixel Streaming) are set out in the note, along with the fact
    that Pixel Streaming is the only one delivering the literal request and the
    only one spec section 05's zero-idle-cost principle rules out.

Immediate driver: the approval workflow is reachable as of Phase 1 but still
blind -- an approver sees file metadata and cannot look at the bytes. The
recommended starting point (the note's layer B: ZIP + manifest download) fixes
that and is needed under every answer to the open questions.

